<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Argishti I</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">r. c. 786–764 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/8th-century-bce">8th century BCE</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Argishti I was king of Urartu from about 786 to 764 BCE, the son and successor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/menua">Menua</a>. His annals, carved in the rock of Van, record campaigns north into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-caucasus">Transcaucasia</a> and against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/assyrian-empire">Assyrians</a>. In 782 BCE he founded the fortress of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/erebuni">Erebuni</a>, on the site of present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yerevan">Yerevan</a>, and he also built Argishtikhinili in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aras-river">Araxes</a> valley, settling them with captives from his wars.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/argishti-i.jpg" alt="Argishti I" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Proudbolsahye · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Statue_of_Arghishti.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>A cuneiform inscription found in Yerevan records that the city was founded in the summer of 782 BCE by King <b>Argishti I</b> of Urartu, making the capital of the republic one of the world's oldest continuously inhabited cities.</blockquote><a href="/empire/armenia">Armenia · At a glance</a></li><li><blockquote>The polity of Kilkhi or Qulḫa warred first against the Assyrians under Tiglath-Pileser I and then against Urartu under <b>Argishti I</b> and Menua, and Sarduri II of Urartu claimed a victory over it in the mid-8th century BCE.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-colchis">Kingdom of Colchis · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Yerevan, capital of modern Armenia, traces its origin to the Urartian fortress of Erebuni, founded by <b>Argishti I</b> in 782 BCE, and celebrated its 2,750th anniversary in 1968.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-urartu">Kingdom of Urartu · At a glance</a></li><li><blockquote>Diauehi was worn down by repeated campaigns of the Kingdom of Urartu under Menua and <b>Argishti I</b>, and its king Utupurshi was forced into tribute.</blockquote><a href="/state/diauehi">Diauehi</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Argishti I</b> of the Kingdom of Urartu conquered much of Etiuni in a series of campaigns in the early 8th century BCE, and his successor Sarduri II campaigned there again.</blockquote><a href="/state/etiuni">Etiuni</a></li></ul></section>
